# Wire the generated API

This generator deliberately supports JSON OpenAPI 3.0 parameterless GETs and
local component DTOs. It fails on unsupported constructs rather than guessing.
The integration skill handles richer contracts manually. Inspect securitySchemes:
authenticated calls need an approved token provider and bounded single-flight
refresh; this skeleton intentionally installs no credentials or interceptors.

Apply `org.jetbrains.kotlin.plugin.serialization` matching the project's Kotlin
version. Add reviewed compatible `com.squareup.retrofit2:retrofit`,
`com.squareup.retrofit2:converter-kotlinx-serialization`, `com.squareup.okhttp3:okhttp`,
and `org.jetbrains.kotlinx:kotlinx-serialization-json` catalog entries. Keep
Retrofit and its converter at the same version. Validate against official
Square and Kotlin release documentation before resolving dependencies.

Place these files under the selected module's source package, add INTERNET to
the manifest, and bind `createApi` and `ApiRepository` via the project's DI.
Provide an HTTPS base URL ending with `/` and an OkHttpClient with explicit
timeouts and no body/token logging in release. Map success DTOs to domain models
with `map` at the repository boundary; provide product-specific error messages.
Failure causes remain internal for diagnosis; the safe `toString` exposes only
kind/code. Do not forward raw exception text or causes into UI/telemetry without
an approved redaction and data policy.
Test decoding, 401, 403, 429, 500, malformed JSON, IO failure, and cancellation
using MockWebServer. Copy `tests/ApiResultTest.kt` to the module's JVM test source
set and add JUnit plus kotlinx-coroutines-test matching the project. The emitted
tests cover transport error translation and cancellation propagation; add
contract-specific success/HTTP/decoding tests for the actual backend.
This output is generated source, not a tested integration.
